{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`fsck` is a DIAGNOSTIC tool: finding problems is its purpose and its success condition. The MCP\ntool nevertheless converts a completed report into `command_failed`, so the caller receives an\nerror object with the entire report stuffed into a message STRING rather than a structured\nresult.\n\n## Mechanism (runtime-confirmed — see the RCA comment)\n\n- `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py:242-243` — the CLI exits non-zero when `issue_count > 0`.\n  **Correct; NOT the defect.**\n- `src/rebar/_lib_reads.py:347-353` — calls `fsck_cli([])` (TEXT mode, so it never asks for the\n  JSON the CLI can already produce) and raises `RebarError(f\"rebar fsck failed (exit {rc}): ...\")`\n  on ANY non-zero rc, flattening the report into an exception message. **This is the defect.**\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py:385,396` — `def fsck(...) -> str` (so no `outputSchema`) returning\n  `rebar.fsck(...)`, so that raise reaches the client as\n  `{\"error\":\"command_failed\",\"exit_code\":1,\"message\":\"<whole report>\"}`.\n\nVerified: on a store with findings, `rebar fsck --output json` returns rc 1 AND a structured\n`{\"issues\":[{\"kind\",\"ticket_id\",\"detail\"}],\"fixed\":[],\"issue_count\":2}` — while `rebar.fsck()`\nraises on the same store. The structure exists one layer down and is discarded.\n\nfsck's rc vocabulary separates clean (`0`) from issues-found (`1`); `rc != 0` collapses them.\nNOTE (corrected during implementation): an uninitialised store also exits `1`, not `2` — see the\n\"Second defect found by the held-out oracle\" comment for the exact discriminator used instead.\n\n## What is EXPECTED and what is the BUG\n\n**Expected: the CLI exiting non-zero on findings.** Conventional for an integrity checker\n(`git fsck`, `fsck(8)`); scripts depend on it. Not a defect. **The bug is the library/MCP layer\ntreating that as a FAILED CALL.**\n\n## Plan\n\n1. **`src/rebar/_lib_reads.py` — new `fsck_report(*, recover=False, report_only=False,\n   repo_root=None) -> dict`.** Runs the same in-process `fsck_cli`, but passes `--output=json`\n   so the CANONICAL shape comes from the CLI itself (no second implementation, so text and JSON\n   cannot drift). Returns `{issues, fixed, issue_count, returncode}` for a COMPLETED run,\n   findings or not. Raises `RebarError` when fsck could not RUN, so a genuine execution failure\n   stays distinguishable from a completed run with findings. The `recover=True` path has no\n   `--output json` in `fsck_recover_cli`, so its text is put through the SAME shared\n   `_transform_json` transform for a uniform shape.\n2. **`rebar.fsck()` is left exactly as it is** — `-> str`, raising on non-zero. It has ~10\n   existing callers across `tests/interfaces/**` that assert on its string return; changing it\n   would break them, and nothing requires it.\n3. **`src/rebar/_mcp_models.py` — add `FsckOut`** mirroring `src/rebar/schemas/fsck.schema.json`\n   (which ALREADY EXISTS, registered as `schemas.FSCK`), following the `_Out` /\n   `ClarityResultOut` / `BridgeFsckOut` pattern its neighbours use.\n4. **`src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py` — `fsck` returns `FsckOut`** built from `rebar.fsck_report()`, so\n   the tool advertises an `outputSchema` like every read tool registered around it. `recover` and\n   `report_only`/`REBAR_MCP_READONLY` handling is unchanged.\n5. **`src/rebar/schemas/fsck.schema.json`** — declare `returncode` as an OPTIONAL property (kept\n   out of `required` so the CLI's own `--output json` payload still validates unchanged), and\n   register `FsckOut -> fsck` in `check_mcp_models.MODEL_SCHEMAS` so the CI drift gate covers it.\n6. **AC4 (`src/rebar/_commands/fsck_scan.py`)** — `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED` stops incrementing the\n   issue counter; the line and its `issues[]` entry are unchanged. Reasoning, evidence, and the\n   deliberate-behaviour-change treatment are in the AC4 decision comment.\n7. **Docs**: regenerate `docs/mcp-reference.md`; document the new counted-ness in\n   `docs/user-guide.md` beside the `TRACKER_DIRTY_TMP_EVENT` precedent and on `docs/concurrency.md`\n   I8.\n\n## Test plan\n\nRED first, held out from the implementer:\n`tests/interfaces/facades/test_fsck_structured_326f.py` builds a store WITH findings and asserts\nthe MCP-layer call returns an enumerable structured result (findings addressable by `kind` and\n`ticket_id`) rather than raising; that `returncode` distinguishes 0/1; that a store fsck cannot\nRUN against still raises; and that `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED` is emitted but not counted. Mutation-\nchecked by re-seeding the confirmed defect (restoring the `rc != 0` raise) and confirming RED.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] The MCP `fsck` tool returns a STRUCTURED result (findings enumerable by kind and ticket)\n      for a completed run, whatever the findings, and advertises an `outputSchema`.\n- [x] A genuine execution failure (fsck could not run, rc not in 0|1) remains distinguishable\n      from a completed run that found issues.\n- [x] The CLI's non-zero-on-findings exit is PRESERVED — scripts depend on it; only the\n      library/MCP surfacing changes.\n- [x] `rebar.fsck()`'s existing `-> str`/raise contract is unchanged for its existing callers.\n- [x] Decided and recorded whether self-resolved `STATUS_FORK_RESOLVED` entries count toward the\n      failure exit; the CLI's exit reflects that decision.\n\n## Reported by\n\nA parallel Copilot CLI session against the deployed server.\n\n- [ ] **LIVE VALIDATION — REQUIRED TO CLOSE.** `fsck` called through the deployed endpoint returns a STRUCTURED result with enumerable findings, not `command_failed`.\n      Evidence must be OBSERVED against the deployed server at\n      `https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp` and recorded in a ticket comment naming\n      the tool(s) called, the arguments, and the actual response. A passing local test is\n      NOT sufficient, and neither is a merged change: the container must have redeployed\n      and served the call. This exists because a fix here was previously reported complete\n      on the strength of a merge while the deployed behaviour was still broken.\n"}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787864610262913003,"uuid":"aa478492-302d-41ac-bedd-a9243e5805ac"}